-- Q1 [Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization · easy · Skill 1 · general-chemistry · ans A] (g67)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization',
    $qt67$In gaseous beryllium chloride, \(\text{BeCl}_2\), the central beryllium atom forms two \(\text{Be}-\text{Cl}\) bonds and carries no lone pairs. Based on the hybridization of this central atom, the molecule is most likely to adopt which molecular geometry?$qt67$,
    $op67$[{"label":"A","text":"Linear, with a \\(\\text{Cl}-\\text{Be}-\\text{Cl}\\) bond angle of \\(180^\\circ\\), because beryllium is \\(sp\\) hybridized"},{"label":"B","text":"Bent, with a \\(\\text{Cl}-\\text{Be}-\\text{Cl}\\) bond angle near \\(109.5^\\circ\\), because beryllium is \\(sp^3\\) hybridized"},{"label":"C","text":"Trigonal planar, with \\(\\text{Cl}-\\text{Be}-\\text{Cl}\\) bond angles of \\(120^\\circ\\), because beryllium is \\(sp^2\\) hybridized"},{"label":"D","text":"Bent, with a \\(\\text{Cl}-\\text{Be}-\\text{Cl}\\) bond angle near \\(120^\\circ\\), because beryllium is \\(sp^2\\) hybridized"}]$op67$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex67$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because the number of regions of electron density around the central atom fixes both its hybridization and the molecule's geometry. In \(\text{BeCl}_2\), beryllium is surrounded by exactly two regions of electron density (the two \(\text{Be}-\text{Cl}\) bonding regions) and has no lone pairs. Two electron-density regions require two equivalent hybrid orbitals, formed by mixing one \(s\) and one \(p\) orbital, so beryllium is \(sp\) hybridized. Two \(sp\) hybrid orbitals point in opposite directions to minimize electron–electron repulsion, giving a linear arrangement with a \(\text{Cl}-\text{Be}-\text{Cl}\) bond angle of \(180^\circ\). (Choice B) An \(sp^3\) hybridization with a \(\sim 109.5^\circ\) angle arises from four regions of electron density, not two; assigning four regions to a central atom that has only two bonds and no lone pairs overcounts the electron-density regions. (Choice C) An \(sp^2\) hybridization corresponds to three regions of electron density and yields a trigonal planar shape only when all three regions are bonding; with just two \(\text{Be}-\text{Cl}\) bonds and no lone pairs there are only two regions, so the geometry is not trigonal planar. (Choice D) A bent geometry with a \(\sim 120^\circ\) angle requires three regions of electron density of which one is a lone pair; because beryllium in \(\text{BeCl}_2\) has no lone pair, no region is available to bend the molecule away from linear. Skill 1$ex67$,
    'easy', '5B', $cc67$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc67$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a67B$assigns sp3 hybridization and a tetrahedral-derived bent angle to a two-region central atom by overcounting electron-density regions$a67B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a67C$applies the three-region sp2 trigonal planar result to a central atom that has only two bonding regions$a67C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a67D$treats one of only two bonding regions as if accompanied by a lone pair, producing a bent sp2 geometry from a linear case$a67D$ FROM q;

-- Q2 [Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans C] (g68)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization',
    $qt68$Ethyne, \(\text{HC}\equiv\text{CH}\), contains a carbon-carbon triple bond and two terminal \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bonds. How many \(\sigma\) bonds and how many \(\pi\) bonds does this molecule contain?$qt68$,
    $op68$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(2\\) \\(\\sigma\\) bonds and \\(3\\) \\(\\pi\\) bonds"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(3\\) \\(\\sigma\\) bonds and \\(3\\) \\(\\pi\\) bonds"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(3\\) \\(\\sigma\\) bonds and \\(2\\) \\(\\pi\\) bonds"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(5\\) \\(\\sigma\\) bonds and \\(2\\) \\(\\pi\\) bonds"}]$op68$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex68$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because the \(\text{C}\equiv\text{C}\) triple bond is one \(\sigma\) plus two \(\pi\) bonds, and adding the two \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) single bonds gives \(3\) \(\sigma\) and \(2\) \(\pi\) total. Any single bond between two atoms is always a \(\sigma\) bond formed by head-on orbital overlap; a multiple bond consists of exactly one \(\sigma\) bond plus one \(\pi\) bond for each additional shared pair, where each \(\pi\) bond arises from sideways overlap of unhybridized \(p\) orbitals. In ethyne each carbon is \(sp\) hybridized, using one \(sp\) hybrid for the \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) \(\sigma\) bond and one for the \(\text{C}-\text{C}\) \(\sigma\) bond, leaving two unhybridized \(p\) orbitals on each carbon to form the two \(\pi\) bonds of the triple bond. (Choice A) Counting only the two \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bonds as \(\sigma\) and assigning all three lines of the triple bond to \(\pi\) bonds gives \(2\) \(\sigma\) and \(3\) \(\pi\), but the first bond of any multiple bond must be a \(\sigma\) bond. (Choice B) Treating the triple bond as three \(\pi\) bonds while still counting three \(\sigma\) bonds double-assigns the central bond, since the triple bond contributes one \(\sigma\) and only two \(\pi\) bonds. (Choice D) Counting every one of the five bond lines as a \(\sigma\) bond in addition to the two \(\pi\) bonds overcounts the framework; the two \(\pi\) bonds are part of the triple bond, not separate from its single \(\sigma\) bond. Skill 2$ex68$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc68$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc68$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a68A$Assigns every line of the triple bond to a pi bond, forgetting that the first bond of any multiple bond is always a sigma bond$a68A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a68B$Correctly counts three sigma bonds but treats the triple bond as three pi bonds, double-counting the central sigma as both sigma and pi$a68B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a68D$Correctly identifies two pi bonds but counts all five bonding lines as sigma bonds, overcounting the sigma framework$a68D$ FROM q;

-- Q3 [Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans B] (g69)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization',
    $qt69$At \(25\,^{\circ}\text{C}\), \(cis\text{-}2\text{-butene}\) does not interconvert with \(trans\text{-}2\text{-butene}\), so the two methyl groups stay locked on fixed sides of the \(\text{C}=\text{C}\). Which feature of the double bond is most directly responsible for this restricted rotation?$qt69$,
    $op69$[{"label":"A","text":"The \\(sp^2\\) hybridization of the alkene carbons makes the \\(\\sigma\\) framework itself too rigid to twist."},{"label":"B","text":"Rotation about the \\(\\text{C}=\\text{C}\\) axis would force the two parallel \\(p\\) orbitals out of alignment, breaking the \\(\\pi\\)-bond overlap at a large energy cost."},{"label":"C","text":"The \\(\\sigma\\) bond locks the carbons in place while the \\(\\pi\\) bond freely permits the two ends to rotate relative to each other."},{"label":"D","text":"Steric clash between the two methyl groups, rather than any orbital overlap, prevents the carbons from rotating past each other."}]$op69$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex69$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because the \(\pi\) bond is formed by side-on overlap of two parallel unhybridized \(p\) orbitals, and twisting about the \(\text{C}=\text{C}\) axis misaligns those orbitals and destroys the overlap, which costs roughly the energy of the \(\pi\) bond and therefore blocks rotation at ordinary temperatures. In a \(\text{C}=\text{C}\) double bond the first bond is a \(\sigma\) bond (cylindrically symmetric about the internuclear axis, so it alone would allow free rotation) and the second is a \(\pi\) bond whose two lobes lie above and below the molecular plane; because intact \(\pi\) overlap demands that the \(p\) orbitals stay parallel, the four substituents on the two alkene carbons are held coplanar and the \(cis\)/\(trans\) arrangement is locked in. (Choice A) The \(sp^2\) hybridization fixes the trigonal-planar geometry and the bond angles, but a single \(\sigma\) bond between \(sp^2\) carbons (as in a \(\text{C}-\text{C}\) single bond) permits essentially free rotation; \(\sigma\) symmetry does not by itself restrict twisting, so hybridization is not the direct cause. (Choice C) This reverses the roles of the two bonds: the \(\sigma\) bond is the one whose cylindrical symmetry would allow rotation, whereas it is the \(\pi\) bond that resists it, so attributing the locking to \(\sigma\) and free rotation to \(\pi\) inverts the actual relationship. (Choice D) Although the methyl groups do experience some steric interaction, restricted rotation about \(\text{C}=\text{C}\) arises even in simple alkenes with no bulky substituents, so the barrier is the energetic cost of breaking \(\pi\) overlap rather than steric clash. Skill 2$ex69$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc69$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc69$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a69A$sp2-sigma-framework-credited-for-rigidity$a69A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a69C$sigma-restricts-pi-rotates role swap$a69C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a69D$steric-clash-substituted-for-pi-overlap-barrier$a69D$ FROM q;

-- Q4 [Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization · easy · Skill 1 · general-chemistry · ans D] (g70)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization',
    $qt70$In gaseous boron trichloride \(\text{BCl}_3\), the central boron atom forms three bonds to chlorine and carries no lone pairs. Which hybridization is most consistent with this bonding arrangement on boron?$qt70$,
    $op70$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(sp^3d\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(sp\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(sp^3\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(sp^2\\)"}]$op70$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex70$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because the boron atom in \(\text{BCl}_3\) is surrounded by three bonding regions and no lone pairs, giving three regions of electron density. Three electron regions are accommodated by mixing one \(s\) orbital with two \(p\) orbitals to form three equivalent \(sp^2\) hybrid orbitals, which point toward the corners of a trigonal-planar arrangement with \(\text{Cl-B-Cl}\) angles of about \(120^\circ\). (Choice A) \(sp^3d\) hybridization corresponds to five regions of electron density, more than the three present on boron. (Choice B) \(sp\) hybridization corresponds to only two regions of electron density, fewer than the three bonding regions on boron. (Choice C) \(sp^3\) hybridization corresponds to four regions of electron density; boron has only three bonds and no lone pairs, so a fourth region is not present. Skill 1$ex70$,
    'easy', '5B', $cc70$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc70$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'scale_unit_error', $a70A$Overcounts electron regions, assigning a higher hybridization than the three regions present$a70A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a70B$Undercounts electron regions, treating the molecule as having only two bonding regions$a70B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a70C$Defaults to the more familiar four-region sp3 case despite boron having only three bonds and no lone pairs$a70C$ FROM q;

-- Q5 [Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization · hard · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans A] (g71)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization',
    $qt71$In acetamide, \(\text{CH}_3\text{-C(=O)-NH}_2\), the methyl carbon forms only single bonds while the carbonyl carbon carries the \(\text{C=O}\) double bond. What is the hybridization of the methyl carbon and the carbonyl carbon, respectively?$qt71$,
    $op71$[{"label":"A","text":"Methyl C is \\(sp^3\\); carbonyl C is \\(sp^2\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"Methyl C is \\(sp^2\\); carbonyl C is \\(sp^3\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"Methyl C is \\(sp^3\\); carbonyl C is \\(sp^3\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"Methyl C is \\(sp^3\\); carbonyl C is \\(sp\\)"}]$op71$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex71$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because the methyl carbon has four single-bond electron domains (\(sp^3\)) while the carbonyl carbon has three electron domains around a \(\text{C=O}\) double bond (\(sp^2\)). Hybridization is set by the number of sigma-bond plus lone-pair domains on an atom: the methyl carbon forms three \(\text{C-H}\) sigma bonds and one \(\text{C-C}\) sigma bond for four domains, requiring four equivalent \(sp^3\) hybrids in a tetrahedral arrangement; the carbonyl carbon forms three sigma bonds (to \(\text{CH}_3\), to \(\text{N}\), and to \(\text{O}\)) plus one pi bond to oxygen, and because the pi bond uses an unhybridized \(p\) orbital rather than a hybrid orbital, only the three sigma domains count, giving \(sp^2\) trigonal planar geometry. (Choice B) reverses the assignments, placing \(sp^2\) on the carbon with four single bonds and \(sp^3\) on the carbon bearing the double bond, which contradicts the domain count for each center. (Choice C) treats the carbonyl carbon as \(sp^3\) by overlooking that a \(\text{C=O}\) double bond reduces the number of hybrid-orbital domains to three. (Choice D) counts the pi bond of the \(\text{C=O}\) double bond as an additional hybridized electron domain, yielding two domains and \(sp\); however, the pi bond resides in an unhybridized \(p\) orbital and does not add a hybrid domain, so the carbonyl carbon remains \(sp^2\). Skill 2$ex71$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc71$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc71$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a71B$Swaps the sp3/sp2 assignments between the single-bonded carbon and the double-bonded carbon$a71B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a71C$Assigns sp3 to every carbon, ignoring that a C=O double bond lowers the carbonyl carbon to sp2$a71C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a71D$Counts the pi bond as a separate hybrid electron domain, over-reducing the carbonyl carbon to sp$a71D$ FROM q;

-- Q6 [Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans B] (g72)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization',
    $qt72$Hydrogen cyanide has the connectivity \(\text{H}-\text{C}\equiv\text{N}\), so its carbon atom is bonded to a hydrogen and triple-bonded to nitrogen and bears no lone pairs. Applying valence bond theory to this carbon, its hybridization and the geometric arrangement of the atoms around it are most likely$qt72$,
    $op72$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(sp^2\\) hybridized, with a trigonal planar arrangement and an \\(\\text{H}-\\text{C}-\\text{N}\\) angle near \\(120^\\circ\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(sp\\) hybridized, with a linear arrangement and an \\(\\text{H}-\\text{C}-\\text{N}\\) angle of \\(180^\\circ\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(sp^3\\) hybridized, with a tetrahedral arrangement and an \\(\\text{H}-\\text{C}-\\text{N}\\) angle near \\(109.5^\\circ\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(sp\\) hybridized, with a bent arrangement and an \\(\\text{H}-\\text{C}-\\text{N}\\) angle near \\(120^\\circ\\)."}]$op72$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex72$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because the carbon in hydrogen cyanide has only two regions of electron density: one \(\sigma\) bond to hydrogen and one \(\sigma\) bond to nitrogen (the triple bond \(\text{C}\equiv\text{N}\) counts as a single region, comprising one \(\sigma\) and two \(\pi\) bonds), with no lone pairs on carbon. In valence bond theory, two electron domains require two equivalent hybrid orbitals, which are formed by mixing one \(s\) and one \(p\) atomic orbital to give two \(sp\) hybrids; the two remaining unhybridized \(p\) orbitals on carbon form the two \(\pi\) bonds of the triple bond. Two \(sp\) hybrids orient \(180^\circ\) apart to minimize electron-pair repulsion, producing a linear arrangement, so the \(\text{H}-\text{C}-\text{N}\) atoms lie in a straight line at \(180^\circ\). (Choice A) Trigonal planar geometry and \(sp^2\) hybridization arise from three regions of electron density, but counting three domains on this carbon overcounts the single \(\text{C}\equiv\text{N}\) region as more than one, so the \(\sim120^\circ\) angle does not apply. (Choice C) Tetrahedral geometry with \(sp^3\) hybridization corresponds to four electron domains; treating each of the three bonds within the triple bond as a separate region inflates the domain count to four and incorrectly yields \(\sim109.5^\circ\). (Choice D) The \(sp\) hybridization is correctly identified, but a bent shape with a \(\sim120^\circ\) angle would require a lone pair or additional domain on carbon to compress the geometry; carbon here has no lone pair, so two \(sp\) hybrids remain \(180^\circ\) apart and the arrangement is linear, not bent. Skill 2$ex72$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc72$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc72$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a72A$triple bond miscounted as two electron regions, giving three domains and sp2 trigonal planar$a72A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a72C$each of the three bonds in the triple bond counted as a separate domain, giving four domains and sp3 tetrahedral$a72C$ FROM q
