-- Q8 [Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization · hard · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans D] (g74)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization',
    $qt74$Acetonitrile has the connectivity \( \text{H}_3\text{C-C} \equiv \text{N} \), in which a methyl carbon is single-bonded to a second carbon that is triple-bonded to a terminal nitrogen. Counting every bond in the molecule, how many \( \sigma \) (sigma) bonds and how many \( \pi \) (pi) bonds does acetonitrile contain?$qt74$,
    $op74$[{"label":"A","text":"\\( 4\\ \\sigma \\) bonds and \\( 3\\ \\pi \\) bonds"},{"label":"B","text":"\\( 6\\ \\sigma \\) bonds and \\( 2\\ \\pi \\) bonds"},{"label":"C","text":"\\( 5\\ \\sigma \\) bonds and \\( 3\\ \\pi \\) bonds"},{"label":"D","text":"\\( 5\\ \\sigma \\) bonds and \\( 2\\ \\pi \\) bonds"}]$op74$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex74$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because every single bond is one \( \sigma \) bond, and a triple bond is always one \( \sigma \) bond plus two \( \pi \) bonds. In acetonitrile the three C-H bonds contribute three \( \sigma \) bonds, the \( \text{C-C} \) single bond contributes one \( \sigma \) bond, and the \( \text{C} \equiv \text{N} \) triple bond contributes one \( \sigma \) bond and two \( \pi \) bonds, for a total of \( 3 + 1 + 1 = 5\ \sigma \) bonds and \( 2\ \pi \) bonds. The OpenStax treatment of valence bond theory specifies that the first bond between two atoms is always a \( \sigma \) bond (end-to-end orbital overlap) and that any additional bonds in a multiple bond are \( \pi \) bonds (side-by-side overlap of unhybridized p orbitals); a triple bond is therefore one \( \sigma \) and two \( \pi \). (Choice A) This counts the \( \text{C} \equiv \text{N} \) triple bond as three \( \pi \) bonds and drops one \( \sigma \) bond, which contradicts the rule that the first bond of any multiple bond is a \( \sigma \) bond, so a triple bond is one \( \sigma \) plus two \( \pi \), not three \( \pi \). (Choice B) This treats the connectivity as containing a separate \( \text{C-N} \) linkage in addition to the \( \text{C} \equiv \text{N} \) triple bond, double-counting one \( \sigma \) bond; the triple bond's single \( \sigma \) component is the only carbon-nitrogen \( \sigma \) bond present. (Choice C) This correctly tallies the five \( \sigma \) bonds but assigns three \( \pi \) bonds to the triple bond, whereas a triple bond contains exactly two \( \pi \) bonds because only two unhybridized p orbitals on each atom are available for side-by-side overlap. Skill 2$ex74$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc74$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc74$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
