UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a77C$Correctly identifies dispersion as present in both molecules but wrongly concludes it is the distinguishing force, overlooking the permanent dipole$a77C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a77D$Upgrades a partial charge on a polar covalent bond to a full ionic charge, invoking ion–dipole forces where only dipole–dipole applies$a77D$ FROM q;

-- Q12 [Intermolecular Forces · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans C] (g78)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Intermolecular Forces',
    $qt78$Water (\(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), molar mass \(18\ \text{g/mol}\)) boils at \(100\,^\circ\text{C}\), whereas the heavier \(\text{H}_2\text{S}\) (molar mass \(34\ \text{g/mol}\)) boils at \(-60\,^\circ\text{C}\). Given that both are bent molecules, the dominant intermolecular force that most likely accounts for water's far higher boiling point is$qt78$,
    $op78$[{"label":"A","text":"stronger London dispersion forces in \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\), because its lower molar mass concentrates its electron cloud and raises its polarizability."},{"label":"B","text":"a more polar S-H bond than O-H bond, which gives \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{S}\\) dipole-dipole forces that water lacks."},{"label":"C","text":"hydrogen bonding between water molecules, made possible because H is bonded to the small, highly electronegative O atom."},{"label":"D","text":"the higher molar mass of \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{S}\\), which increases its dipole-dipole attractions above those of \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\)."}]$op78$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex78$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because the O-H bonds of water let each molecule hydrogen bond through H attached to a small, highly electronegative O, an interaction far stronger than any force available to \(\text{H}_2\text{S}\). Hydrogen bonding requires an H atom covalently bonded to N, O, or F; oxygen's high electronegativity and small atomic radius create a large bond dipole and allow close approach of the partially positive H to a lone pair on a neighboring O, producing an exceptionally strong dipole-dipole attraction. Sulfur is much less electronegative and larger, so \(\text{H}_2\text{S}\) cannot hydrogen bond and is held together mainly by weak dipole-dipole and dispersion forces; this is why water boils \(160\,^\circ\text{C}\) higher despite being the lighter molecule, since hydrogen bonding overwhelms the modest rise in dispersion forces expected from greater molar mass. (Choice A) London dispersion forces increase with greater molar mass and a larger, more polarizable electron cloud, so the heavier \(\text{H}_2\text{S}\) actually has stronger dispersion forces than \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\); a lower molar mass does not concentrate the electron cloud to raise polarizability. (Choice B) The S-H bond is less polar than the O-H bond because sulfur is less electronegative than oxygen, so this misstates the relative bond polarity and incorrectly denies that the more polar molecule, water, has dipole-dipole forces. (Choice D) Higher molar mass strengthens dispersion forces rather than dipole-dipole attractions, and even so the increase does not exceed water's hydrogen bonding; attributing water's higher boiling point to a property that favors \(\text{H}_2\text{S}\) reverses the actual trend. Skill 2$ex78$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc78$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc78$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
