-- Q14 [Intermolecular Forces · easy · Skill 1 · general-chemistry · ans D] (g80)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Intermolecular Forces',
    $qt80$In liquid water, each \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) molecule is attracted to neighboring \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) molecules through hydrogen bonding. What is the nature of the hydrogen bond linking one water molecule to the next?$qt80$,
    $op80$[{"label":"A","text":"A covalent bond, in which the hydrogen of one \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\) shares an electron pair with the oxygen of the adjacent \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"An ionic bond, formed by complete transfer of an electron from the hydrogen of one \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\) to the oxygen of the adjacent \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"A nonpolar London dispersion interaction arising from momentary fluctuations in the electron clouds of the two \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\) molecules."},{"label":"D","text":"An intermolecular electrostatic attraction between the partially positive H of one \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\) and a lone pair on the partially negative O of the adjacent \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\)."}]$op80$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex80$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because a hydrogen bond is an intermolecular electrostatic attraction between a partially positive H (bonded to N, O, or F) on one molecule and a lone pair on a partially negative N, O, or F on another molecule, not a shared-electron-pair bond. Within each \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), the large electronegativity difference between O and H makes the O-H bonds polar, leaving each H with a partial positive charge (\(\delta^+\)) and the O with a partial negative charge (\(\delta^-\)); the \(\delta^+\) hydrogen of one molecule is then drawn to a lone pair on the \(\delta^-\) oxygen of a neighbor, which is simply a strong dipole-type attraction between separate molecules rather than a new chemical bond holding atoms together within a molecule. (Choice A) Hydrogen bonding does not share an electron pair between the two molecules; the H retains its covalent bond to its own oxygen, and the attraction to the neighboring oxygen is an intermolecular force, not a covalent bond. (Choice B) No electron is transferred and no ions are produced between the two water molecules; the interaction is a partial-charge attraction between intact neutral molecules, not an ionic bond. (Choice C) London dispersion forces stem from instantaneous, induced dipoles in nonpolar regions and are far weaker than hydrogen bonding, which instead relies on the permanent O-H bond dipoles and the oxygen lone pair. Skill 1$ex80$,
    'easy', '5B', $cc80$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc80$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
