-- Q15 [Intermolecular Forces · hard · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans B] (g81)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Intermolecular Forces',
    $qt81$Decane (\(\text{C}_{10}\text{H}_{22}\), a nonpolar straight-chain alkane) boils at \(174\,^\circ\text{C}\), whereas the polar molecule chloromethane (\(\text{CH}_3\text{Cl}\)) boils at \(-24\,^\circ\text{C}\). What most likely accounts for the far higher boiling point of the nonpolar species?$qt81$,
    $op81$[{"label":"A","text":"Decane molecules engage in hydrogen bonding through their many C-H bonds, which chloromethane cannot do."},{"label":"B","text":"Decane's much larger, more polarizable electron cloud produces London dispersion forces that exceed the dipole-dipole attractions in chloromethane."},{"label":"C","text":"Chloromethane is held together by weak ionic attractions between \\(\\text{C}\\) and \\(\\text{Cl}\\), which break more easily than decane's covalent bonds."},{"label":"D","text":"Because chloromethane is polar, its permanent dipole always generates stronger total intermolecular attraction than any dispersion force, so its higher boiling point reflects experimental error."}]$op81$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex81$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because decane's large, extended electron cloud is highly polarizable, generating London dispersion forces strong enough to outweigh the permanent dipole-dipole attraction in the much smaller chloromethane. London dispersion forces arise from instantaneous, induced dipoles and scale with molecular size and polarizability; a \(\text{C}_{10}\) alkane has far more electrons and surface area for transient dipoles than \(\text{CH}_3\text{Cl}\), so even though it lacks a permanent dipole, the cumulative dispersion attraction dominates and raises the boiling point well above that of the smaller polar molecule. (Choice A) Hydrogen bonding requires \(\text{H}\) bonded to \(\text{N}\), \(\text{O}\), or \(\text{F}\); C-H bonds are nonpolar and do not participate in hydrogen bonding, so this attraction is not present in decane. (Choice C) The \(\text{C-Cl}\) bond in chloromethane is a polar covalent bond, not an ionic attraction, and intermolecular forces (not the intramolecular bonds) are what break during boiling, so this misidentifies both the bonding and the process. (Choice D) A permanent dipole does not guarantee a larger total attraction; dispersion forces can exceed dipole-dipole forces when one molecule is much larger and more polarizable, so the data reflect real physical behavior rather than error. Skill 2$ex81$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc81$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc81$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
