-- Q17 [Stereochemistry · easy · Skill 1 · organic-chemistry · ans C] (g83)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Stereochemistry',
    $qt83$2-Bromobutane, \(\text{CH}_3\text{-CHBr-CH}_2\text{-CH}_3\), has exactly one carbon bonded to four different groups. Which carbon is that stereocenter?$qt83$,
    $op83$[{"label":"A","text":"C1, the terminal \\(\\text{CH}_3\\) adjacent to the brominated carbon"},{"label":"B","text":"C3, the \\(\\text{CH}_2\\) group between the brominated carbon and the terminal methyl"},{"label":"C","text":"C2, the carbon bearing \\(\\text{Br}\\), bonded to \\(\\text{H}\\), \\(\\text{Br}\\), a methyl group, and an ethyl group"},{"label":"D","text":"C4, the terminal \\(\\text{CH}_3\\) farthest from the brominated carbon"}]$op83$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex83$This is an Organic Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because C2 carries four nonidentical substituents, \(\text{H}\), \(\text{Br}\), \(\text{CH}_3\) (the C1 side), and \(\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_3\) (the C3-C4 side), which is the defining requirement for a stereocenter. A chiral center is a carbon attached to four different groups, so the molecule must be scanned carbon by carbon: only C2 satisfies the rule, making 2-bromobutane chiral with one stereocenter and a single pair of enantiomers. (Choice A) C1 is a terminal \(\text{CH}_3\) bonded to three hydrogens and one carbon; three identical \(\text{H}\) substituents disqualify it regardless of its position next to the bromine. (Choice B) C3 is bonded to two carbon chains that do differ, but it also carries two hydrogens, and those two identical \(\text{H}\) atoms mean it is bonded to only three distinct groups rather than four. (Choice D) C4 is a terminal \(\text{CH}_3\) with three identical hydrogens; being the carbon farthest from \(\text{Br}\) has no bearing on whether it bonds four different groups. Skill 1$ex83$,
    'easy', '5B', $cc83$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc83$, 'organic-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
