-- Q18 [Stereochemistry · medium · Skill 1 · organic-chemistry · ans A] (g84)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Stereochemistry',
    $qt84$Tartaric acid (2,3-dihydroxybutanedioic acid) has two stereocenters at C2 and C3. Consider the isomer with the \((2R,3R)\) configuration and the isomer with the \((2S,3S)\) configuration. The stereochemical relationship between these two specific isomers is best described as which of the following?$qt84$,
    $op84$[{"label":"A","text":"Enantiomers"},{"label":"B","text":"Diastereomers"},{"label":"C","text":"The same compound in two orientations"},{"label":"D","text":"Constitutional isomers"}]$op84$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex84$This is an Organic Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because the \((2R,3R)\) and \((2S,3S)\) isomers have every stereocenter inverted relative to one another, which makes them nonsuperimposable mirror images, and that is the defining relationship of enantiomers. Tartaric acid has two stereocenters, so its stereoisomers are \((2R,3R)\), \((2S,3S)\), and \((2R,3S)\); the first two are the chiral pair that rotate plane-polarized light in equal and opposite directions, while \((2R,3S)\) is the achiral meso form. Inverting both stereocenters (R→S at C2 and R→S at C3) reflects the entire molecule, so \((2R,3R)\) and \((2S,3S)\) are mirror images that cannot be overlaid, satisfying the definition of enantiomers. (Choice B) Diastereomers are stereoisomers that are not mirror images, such as \((2R,3R)\) compared with the meso \((2R,3S)\) form where only one stereocenter is inverted; because \((2R,3R)\) and \((2S,3S)\) have both centers inverted, they are mirror images and therefore are not diastereomers. (Choice C) The two configurations are not the same compound, because neither one is meso and each has a true nonsuperimposable mirror image; a molecule and its mirror image are identical only when an internal mirror plane makes the molecule achiral, which is not the case for \((2R,3R)\) tartaric acid. (Choice D) Constitutional isomers differ in the connectivity of their atoms, whereas these two isomers share identical bonding and connectivity and differ only in the three-dimensional spatial arrangement at the stereocenters, making them stereoisomers rather than constitutional isomers. Skill 1$ex84$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc84$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc84$, 'organic-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a84B$calls a fully-inverted mirror-image pair diastereomers$a84B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a84C$treats nonsuperimposable mirror images as the identical/superimposable compound$a84C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a84D$labels stereoisomers as constitutional isomers despite identical connectivity$a84D$ FROM q;

-- Q19 [Stereochemistry · hard · Skill 2 · organic-chemistry · ans B] (g85)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Stereochemistry',
    $qt85$Consider the single, pure compound (2R,3S)-2,3-dibromobutane, \(\text{CH}_3\text{CHBrCHBrCH}_3\), in which C2 and C3 are each bonded to four different groups. A sample of this isolated stereoisomer shows no measurable optical rotation. Which of the following best accounts for this observation?$qt85$,
    $op85$[{"label":"A","text":"The sample is optically inactive because it is necessarily a 1:1 racemic mixture of two enantiomers whose rotations cancel."},{"label":"B","text":"The molecule possesses an internal mirror plane that makes it superimposable on its own mirror image, so despite having two stereocenters it is achiral and rotates plane-polarized light by a net of zero."},{"label":"C","text":"Because the molecule contains two stereocenters, it must be chiral and optically active, so the absence of rotation indicates the sample was contaminated or measured incorrectly."},{"label":"D","text":"Rapid rotation about the C2–C3 single bond interconverts conformers whose opposite rotations average to zero over time."}]$op85$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex85$This is an Organic Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because (2R,3S)-2,3-dibromobutane is a meso compound: it contains two stereocenters yet is achiral because an internal mirror plane bisecting the C2–C3 bond relates the (2R) half to the (3S) half, making the molecule superimposable on its mirror image. An achiral molecule cannot rotate plane-polarized light, so a pure sample is optically inactive (internal compensation). Note that 2,3-dibromobutane therefore has only three stereoisomers, not 2^2=4: the (2R,3R)/(2S,3S) pair of enantiomers plus the single meso (2R,3S) form. (Choice A) The sample is a single, pure stereoisomer as stated, not a mixture of two enantiomers; its inactivity arises from an internal mirror plane within one molecule (internal compensation), not from the external averaging of two molecules in a racemate. (Choice C) Having two stereocenters does not guarantee chirality; when an internal mirror plane is present the molecule is meso and achiral, so the absence of rotation is the expected result rather than an artifact. (Choice D) Optical activity is determined by molecular symmetry, not by conformational populations; rotation about a single bond does not convert a chiral molecule into an inactive one, and conformers of a chiral molecule do not have canceling rotations. Skill 2: Reasoning about scientific principles to draw conclusions.$ex85$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc85$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc85$, 'organic-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a85A$confusing internal compensation (a single meso molecule) with external compensation (a racemic mixture)$a85A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a85C$asserting that the presence of stereocenters necessarily implies chirality/optical activity$a85C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a85D$attributing loss of optical activity to conformational averaging about a single bond rather than to molecular symmetry$a85D$ FROM q;

-- Q20 [Stereochemistry · medium · Skill 2 · organic-chemistry · ans D] (g86)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Stereochemistry',
    $qt86$Cis-trans isomerism demands both restricted rotation and two different substituents on each of the relevant carbons. Which molecule satisfies these conditions and therefore exists as distinct cis and trans forms?$qt86$,
    $op86$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(1,2\\text{-dichloroethane}\\), \\(\\text{ClCH}_2\\text{-CH}_2\\text{Cl}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(1,1\\text{-dichloroethene}\\), \\(\\text{Cl}_2\\text{C=CH}_2\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"propene, \\(\\text{CH}_2\\text{=CH-CH}_3\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(2\\text{-butene}\\), \\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{-CH=CH-CH}_3\\)"}]$op86$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex86$This is an Organic Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because each \(sp^2\) carbon of the \(\text{C=C}\) bond in \(2\text{-butene}\) carries two different groups (one \(\text{H}\) and one \(\text{CH}_3\)), so the \(\pi\) bond's restricted rotation locks the two methyl groups into either the same side (cis) or opposite sides (trans). Cis-trans isomerism requires a barrier to rotation, supplied here by the \(\pi\) bond of the double bond, plus two distinct substituents on each doubly bonded carbon so that the two spatial arrangements are non-identical; both criteria are met only in \(2\text{-butene}\). (Choice A) \(1,2\text{-dichloroethane}\) contains only a \(\text{C-C}\) single bond, which permits free rotation, so the differing arrangements are merely interconverting conformers rather than separable geometric isomers. (Choice B) \(1,1\text{-dichloroethene}\) does have a rotation-restricting \(\text{C=C}\) bond, but one carbon bears two identical \(\text{Cl}\) atoms, so swapping them yields the same molecule and no distinct cis and trans forms exist. (Choice C) Propene has a \(\text{C=C}\) bond, yet the terminal carbon carries two identical \(\text{H}\) atoms, which fails the requirement that each doubly bonded carbon hold two different groups. Skill 2$ex86$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc86$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc86$, 'organic-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a86A$Treats free-rotation single-bond conformers as separable cis/trans isomers, ignoring the restricted-rotation requirement$a86A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a86B$Recognizes the restricted-rotation criterion from the C=C bond but overlooks that two identical substituents on one carbon prevent distinct isomers$a86B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a86C$Equates the mere presence of a double bond with cis-trans isomerism, skipping the two-different-groups-per-carbon check$a86C$ FROM q;

-- Q21 [Stereochemistry · hard · Skill 2 · organic-chemistry · ans C] (g87)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Stereochemistry',
    $qt87$A trisubstituted alkene has the connectivity \((\text{HO})(\text{CH}_3)\text{C}=\text{C}(\text{CH}_2\text{Cl})(\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_3)\), and the \(-\text{OH}\) group and the \(-\text{CH}_2\text{Cl}\) group lie on opposite sides of the \(\text{C}=\text{C}\) axis. A chemist must assign the double-bond configuration using CIP priority on each alkene carbon.$qt87$,
    $op87$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(Z\\), because the two carbon-containing substituents \\(-\\text{CH}_3\\) and \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{CH}_3\\) lie on the same side."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(Z\\), because \\(-\\text{OH}\\) outranks \\(-\\text{CH}_3\\) and \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{CH}_3\\) outranks \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{Cl}\\), placing the higher-priority groups on the same side."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(E\\), because \\(-\\text{OH}\\) outranks \\(-\\text{CH}_3\\) and \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{Cl}\\) outranks \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{CH}_3\\), placing the higher-priority groups on opposite sides."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(E\\), because \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{CH}_3\\) is the longest chain and therefore the highest-priority group on its carbon, ranking it opposite the \\(-\\text{OH}\\)."}]$op87$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex87$This is an Organic Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because on the left carbon \(-\text{OH}\) (first atom O) outranks \(-\text{CH}_3\) (first atom C), while on the right carbon \(-\text{CH}_2\text{Cl}\) outranks \(-\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_3\), and these two higher-priority groups sit on opposite sides, defining the \(E\) configuration. By the CIP rules, priority is set by the first point of difference: at the left carbon the choice is decided immediately because O (Z = 8) beats C (Z = 6), so \(-\text{OH}\) wins; at the right carbon both substituents have C as their first atom, so one compares the atoms attached to that carbon — \(-\text{CH}_2\text{Cl}\) presents the set \((\text{Cl}, \text{H}, \text{H})\) whereas \(-\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_3\) presents \((\text{C}, \text{H}, \text{H})\), and Cl (Z = 17) outranks C, so \(-\text{CH}_2\text{Cl}\) wins. With \(-\text{OH}\) and \(-\text{CH}_2\text{Cl}\) on opposite sides of the double bond, the higher-priority groups are trans to one another, which is the definition of \(E\). (Choice A) Configuration is assigned by ranking the two substituents on each carbon, not by tracking whether the carbon-containing groups happen to be on the same side, so grouping \(-\text{CH}_3\) and \(-\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_3\) does not determine \(E\)/\(Z\). (Choice B) The left-carbon ranking is correct, but on the right carbon the comparison \((\text{Cl}, \text{H}, \text{H})\) versus \((\text{C}, \text{H}, \text{H})\) makes \(-\text{CH}_2\text{Cl}\) the higher priority, reversing the conclusion to opposite sides and \(E\). (Choice D) Priority is not decided by chain length; it is decided by the first point of difference in atomic number, where the chlorine on \(-\text{CH}_2\text{Cl}\) outranks the carbon continuation of \(-\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_3\). Skill 2$ex87$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc87$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc87$, 'organic-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
