-- Q22 [Stereochemistry · medium · Skill 1 · organic-chemistry · ans A] (g88)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Stereochemistry',
    $qt88$A sample of pure \((R)\)-2-bromobutane has a measured specific rotation of \(+23.1^\circ\), and an equal number of moles of \((S)\)-2-bromobutane is dissolved into it. What is the observed specific rotation of the resulting solution?$qt88$,
    $op88$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(0^\\circ\\), because the rotations of the two enantiomers are equal in magnitude and opposite in sign"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+23.1^\\circ\\), because the \\((R)\\) enantiomer fixes the sign of the observed rotation"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+46.2^\\circ\\), because the rotations of the two enantiomers add together"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(-23.1^\\circ\\), because the added \\((S)\\) enantiomer rotates light in the opposite direction"}]$op88$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex88$This is an Organic Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because mixing equal moles of \((R)\)- and \((S)\)-2-bromobutane forms a racemic mixture whose components rotate plane-polarized light by equal magnitudes in opposite directions, giving a net rotation of \(0^\circ\). A pair of enantiomers are nonsuperimposable mirror images that are physically identical except in their interaction with plane-polarized light: one rotates the plane by \(+\alpha\) and the other by \(-\alpha\). In a 1:1 (racemic) mixture the contribution of each enantiomer exactly cancels the other, so the sample is optically inactive even though every individual molecule is chiral. (Choice B) The \((R)\) enantiomer does not dominate; an equal amount of the \((S)\) enantiomer is present and its opposite rotation cancels the \((R)\) contribution rather than leaving \(+23.1^\circ\). (Choice C) Equal and opposite rotations subtract to zero rather than adding, so \(+46.2^\circ\) would require two samples rotating light in the same direction. (Choice D) A residual \(-23.1^\circ\) would occur only if the \((S)\) enantiomer were in excess, but the amounts are equal, so the \((R)\) contribution is not left uncanceled in the opposite direction. Skill 1$ex88$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc88$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc88$, 'organic-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a88B$treats one enantiomer as setting the net sign while ignoring the equal opposite contribution$a88B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a88C$adds the two rotation magnitudes instead of recognizing they cancel$a88C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a88D$assumes the added enantiomer leaves a net opposite rotation rather than canceling to zero$a88D$ FROM q;

-- Q23 [Stereochemistry · easy · Skill 1 · organic-chemistry · ans B] (g89)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Stereochemistry',
    $qt89$The open-chain aldopentose 2,3,4-trihydroxypentanal contains three tetrahedral stereocenters, none of which are related to one another by internal symmetry. What is the maximum number of stereoisomers possible for this molecule?$qt89$,
    $op89$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(6\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(8\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(4\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(16\\)"}]$op89$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex89$This is an Organic Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because a molecule with \(n\) stereocenters has a maximum of \(2^n\) stereoisomers, and \(2^3 = 8\). Each tetrahedral stereocenter can adopt either of two configurations (\(R\) or \(S\)), so three independent stereocenters give \(2 \times 2 \times 2 = 2^3 = 8\) possible configurational combinations; because the stem specifies that none of the centers are related by internal symmetry, no combinations collapse into meso forms and the full \(2^n\) maximum is reached. (Choice A) The value \(6\) comes from multiplying the number of stereocenters by two (\(2 \times 3\)) instead of raising two to the power of the number of stereocenters, which treats the relationship as linear rather than exponential. (Choice C) The value \(4\) is \(2^2\), the maximum for a molecule with only two stereocenters, and undercounts by treating the molecule as having one fewer stereocenter than the three given. (Choice D) The value \(16\) is \(2^4\), the maximum for four stereocenters, and overcounts by applying the exponent for one extra stereocenter beyond the three present. Skill 1$ex89$,
    'easy', '5B', $cc89$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc89$, 'organic-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a89A$Multiplying the count of stereocenters by two (2 x 3 = 6) instead of computing 2 raised to the number of stereocenters$a89A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a89C$Applying 2^n with n = 2 instead of n = 3, the maximum for two stereocenters$a89C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a89D$Applying 2^n with n = 4 instead of n = 3, the maximum for four stereocenters$a89D$ FROM q;

-- Q24 [Stereochemistry · medium · Skill 2 · organic-chemistry · ans D] (g90)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Stereochemistry',
    $qt90$Constitutional (structural) isomers share a molecular formula but differ in the connectivity of their atoms, whereas stereoisomers share both formula and connectivity and conformational isomers interconvert by rotation about single bonds. Which of the following pairs of compounds is best classified as a pair of constitutional isomers?$qt90$,
    $op90$[{"label":"A","text":"cis-2-butene and trans-2-butene"},{"label":"B","text":"The anti and gauche rotamers of butane about the C2-C3 bond"},{"label":"C","text":"(R)-2-bromobutane and (S)-2-bromobutane"},{"label":"D","text":"1-propanol and 2-propanol"}]$op90$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex90$This is an Organic Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because 1-propanol and 2-propanol share the molecular formula \(\text{C}_3\text{H}_8\text{O}\) yet differ in how their atoms are connected: in 1-propanol the hydroxyl is bonded to a terminal carbon (\(\mathrm{CH_3CH_2CH_2OH}\)), while in 2-propanol it is bonded to the central carbon (\(\mathrm{CH_3CH(OH)CH_3}\)). Because the bonding sequence itself differs, the pair are constitutional (structural) isomers, not stereoisomers or conformers. Constitutional isomers are defined by identical formula but distinct connectivity, which is precisely what distinguishes them from stereoisomers (same connectivity, different spatial arrangement) and conformational isomers (same molecule sampled at different rotations about single bonds). (Choice A) cis-2-butene and trans-2-butene have identical connectivity (\(\mathrm{CH_3CH{=}CHCH_3}\)) and differ only in the fixed spatial arrangement of groups about the restricted C=C double bond, making them geometric stereoisomers rather than constitutional isomers. (Choice B) The anti and gauche forms of butane are the same molecule with the same connectivity, interconverting freely by rotation about the C2-C3 single bond; they are conformational isomers (rotamers), not constitutional isomers. (Choice C) (R)- and (S)-2-bromobutane have identical connectivity and are nonsuperimposable mirror images, so they are enantiomers, a subtype of stereoisomer, not constitutional isomers. Skill 2: Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving.$ex90$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc90$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc90$, 'organic-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
